	3. Vector:
	Vector is a class in Java that implements the List interface and stores elements in a dynamic array, just like ArrayList, but it is thread-safe.
	
	Imagine a banking application.
	Two employees are updating the same customer record at the same time.
	Without protection:
	Employee A → Adds transaction
Employee B → Removes transaction
	Both modify the list simultaneously.
	This can lead to:
	        • ❌ Wrong data
	        • ❌ Lost updates
	        • ❌ Unexpected errors
	Vector prevents this by allowing only one thread at a time to modify the list.
	
	Synchronization is a mechanism that allows only one thread to execute a critical section of code at a time.
	
	Every operation acquires and releases a lock.
	Lock
	↓
	Perform operation
	↓
	Unlock
	Extra work means extra time.
	That's why Vector is generally slower than ArrayList.
	
